# Emery Plugin

## Overview

This plugin provides the two Emery skills: generate a specification, and build its plan. Each skill is an ultrathin wrapper that invokes the matching `emery` CLI verb and relays its output.

The v1 delivery workflow (plan / refine / execute / status / finalize and the `system-*` definition loop) is archived at git tag `v1`. Its verbs are deleted from the CLI grammar and its skills are deleted from this plugin — do not attempt to run them or reconstruct their behavior by hand. `emery specify` generates `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` from the sources named on the invocation; `emery show spec`, `emery show design`, and `emery show plan` print the reviewable documents of the current revision; `emery build` builds the current plan in waves through one target adapter, and lands the result as one merge commit per slice under the label `emery/<revision>`, verified wave by wave.

## Workflow

```text
/emery:specify <adapter>           (every revision)
/emery:build [<adapter>]           (once the plan is reviewed)
```

That is the whole shipped surface. An `<adapter>` is an exact package reference with a version, `namespace:name@version` (`emery:typescript@0.13.0`); the binary reads it from its store, `~/.emery/adapters`, and fetches a release the store lacks on the first run that names it. The source list never persists — repeat the adapters on every run, or keep them in an operator-owned `emery.toml` selected with `--config [<path>]` (`--config` alone means the project-relative `emery.toml`; a run naming no sources at all discovers the project-root file as a fallback). Every run starts from its sources alone — nothing of the stored revision reaches the synthesis, and requirements number from `REQ-001` in source order. After each run the skill writes `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` (the Markdown projections) beside the code for review. Never hand-edit `.emery/storage` state (the revision store); route through the CLI.

`/emery:build` names one target adapter the same way, or takes it from the `[target]` table of the same `emery.toml`. It builds the committed plan in waves — every slice whose dependencies are merged at once, each in its own working copy, up to `--jobs` at a time — in one repository: a clone of the `[target] repository` kept at `.emery/vcs/repos/<name>` and built from its default branch; with none named, for an adapter that lays a whole tree, a repository the engine lays once at that path and keeps; otherwise the project's own, from its sealed head. The engine seals each slice's changes as one commit, merges them in id order into an integration working copy under the adapter's merge rules — a path the rules leave conflicted is resolved with the model from both sides, the resolution sealed in the slice's working copy over its commit and the slice merged again with it taken whole — has the adapter verify the integrated tree, and labels the verified head `emery/<revision>` there; nothing is pushed. A conflict the model cannot resolve ends the run (`merge-unresolved`), as a wave the adapter does not verify does (`verify-failed`), the label standing at the last verified wave, and a re-run resumes from that label and from the slices the integration working copy holds merged over it rather than starting over, verifying them in their wave with no build turn spent; a turn the model backend's time budget ends is put once more before its failure is the run's. The checkout is never written and the skill commits nothing: it relays the base, the repository, the waves, the merges, and the label, and offers `git log --first-parent`, `git merge`, or a push of the label as the way to take them. A checkout with an uncommitted change outside `.emery/` is refused before any slice is built into it (`base-not-sealed`), so `.emery/` belongs in `.gitignore`. The projections stay `/emery:specify`'s to write, and the build never commits them.

## Tracing and output

One tracing level governs a run — the runtime's host console, the engine, and every adapter alike. A bare run is `info`: the engine's progress at its slow steps and each adapter's seam turn as it opens reach stderr, retries at WARN. Each `-v` raises the level one step and each `-q` lowers it — `-q` warn (progress gone, warnings kept), `-qq` error, `-qqq` off; `-v` debug, `-vv` trace — before or after the verb, and `-v` beside `-q` is a usage error. The flags override a process `RUST_LOG`; a bare run keeps one that is set, so `RUST_LOG=emery_sdk=debug emery specify …` refines a bare run and `RUST_LOG=off` silences it.

**Debug mode**: when the operator explicitly asks for debug (says "debug", or passes `debug` on the slash command), pass `-v` to **every** `emery` subprocess in that skill invocation. A subprocess that must be fully silent passes `-qqq` (or keeps `RUST_LOG=off`) so it does not inherit a raised filter.

Stream roles: the semantic CLI result (text or JSON envelope) is stdout; command failures and live tracing are stderr. Relay the semantic result or structured error once, verbatim; do not repeat or reformat tracing lines in the closing response.

## Troubleshooting

### Common error codes

Every CLI failure prints a kebab-case error code (and usually a `hint:` line — follow it). The codes met most often:

| Error code | What it means | Next action |
|------------|---------------|-------------|
| `specify-source-required` | `emery specify` was run without naming a source, and no project-root `emery.toml` was found | `emery specify <adapter>` (and/or `--description <adapter>=<text>`, or `--config [<path>]`) |
| `build-target-required` | `emery build` was run without naming a target adapter, and no project-root `emery.toml` carries a `[target]` table | `emery build <adapter>`, or add a `[target]` table to `emery.toml` and `emery build --config` |
| `adapter-reference` | An adapter was named without a version or a namespace, or as a path or bare name | Name the exact package reference, `namespace:name@version` |
| `unsupported-version` | The installed binary is older than an adapter's declared minimum `emery-version` | Reinstall through the binary's install channel (the hint prints the literal command), then rerun |
| `spec-not-generated` | `emery show` or `emery build` was run before any revision was committed | `/emery:specify <adapter>` first |
| `repository-required` | `emery build` was run from a directory that is no git repository, through an adapter that builds into the project, and no `[target] repository` was named | `git init` and seal a first commit, or add `repository` to the `[target]` table |
| `base-not-sealed` | The project checkout holds an uncommitted change outside `.emery/` and `.git/`, or has no commit, so there is no sealed base to build from | Commit or stash the paths the message lists (add `.emery/` to `.gitignore`), then rerun |
| `base-not-fresh` | The target adapter lays a whole tree, and the `[target] repository`'s default branch holds a directory at its root outside `.emery/` and `.git/`, which the message names | Drop `repository` from the `[target]` table so the build lays a repository of its own under `.emery/vcs/repos/<name>`, or name an empty one |
| `revision-not-found` | A `[[source]] revision` names nothing its repository holds, a `[target] repository` names no repository or has no default branch, or `.emery/vcs/repos/<name>` already holds a repository the engine laid, which has no origin to fetch | Check the name or URL against the repository; for a taken name, remove the directory or pick another `[target] name`, then rerun |
| `merge-unresolved` | A slice's merge into the integrated tree conflicted at paths the adapter's merge rules leave, and the model could not resolve them: one side removed a path or holds a link, a directory, or a file that is not text there, or the resolution was refused within the available rounds | Inspect the named paths in `.emery/vcs/worktrees/<slice>` and `.emery/vcs/integration`; rerun to build the slice again over the merged head, declare a merge rule in the adapter for a list file or a generated one, or re-run `/emery:specify` for a plan that keeps the two in one slice; the label stands at the last verified wave |
| `verify-failed` | The adapter's verify turn failed a check over the integrated tree after a wave merged | Inspect `.emery/vcs/integration`; rerun to rebuild the wave from the last verified head, or `git bisect` over `emery/<revision>` to attribute the failure to a slice |
| `secret-detected` | A path a slice's build or a wave's verification wrote carries a credential — a key file or `.npmrc` by name, a PEM block or a provider's token by content — so nothing of that change set was sealed | Inspect the named path in `.emery/vcs/worktrees/<slice>` or `.emery/vcs/integration`, remove the credential from where the build read it, then rerun; the label stands at the last verified wave |
| `refused` | The loader rejected the release (an invalid or pre-compiled artifact, a `digest` it does not resolve to, or a namespace the binary routes nowhere) | Read the message; for an unrouted namespace, `wkg get <reference> -o ~/.emery/adapters/` as the hint says |
| `unavailable` | The registry fetch for a release the store lacks failed (network, endpoint, or a missing exact version) | Check connectivity and that the exact version is published under its namespace, then rerun; a copy at `~/.emery/adapters/<namespace>_<name>@<version>.wasm` stands in |
| `spec-outdated` | The stored revision was written under an older grammar than this binary reads | Re-run `/emery:specify <adapter>` to regenerate |

### Reviewing the generated documents

- Read `spec.md` / `design.md` / `plan.md` as re-projected after the run, or run `emery show spec` / `emery show design` / `emery show plan` — the document renders to stdout with its revision id in the front matter
- `spec.md`, `design.md`, and `plan.md` are projections of the stored revision; none is edited by hand — change a source and re-run `/emery:specify`

